              simple: "💬 **Question**: Two Sum problem using a hash map for efficient lookup.\n\n---\n\n⭐️ **Answer**: I can solve this efficiently in one pass by using a hash map to store each number and its index as I loop through the array. For every number, I check if its matching target minus current number is already in my map, and if it is, I return both indices right away.\n\n**🔑 Key Steps**: Initialize an empty hash map to store numbers and their positions. Loop through the array and calculate the difference between the target and the current number. Check if this difference is already in the map, and if so, return its index along with the current index. If not, add the current number and its index to the map and continue.\n\n**💻 Code**:\n```python\ndef twoSum(nums, target):\n    # Map to store the number and its index\n    num_map = {}\n    \n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        # Find the number needed to reach the target\n        complement = target - num\n        \n        # If the complement is already in our map, we found our pair\n        if complement in num_map:\n            return [num_map[complement], i]\n            \n        # Otherwise, add the current number and index to the map\n        num_map[num] = i\n        \n    return []\n```\n\n**💡 Explanation**: This approach checks for the required complement in constant time on average using a hash map, reducing the overall time complexity to linear time, which is Big O of $n$, where $n$ is the number of elements. The space complexity is also linear time, Big O of $n$, because we store up to $n$ elements in the hash map.",
              formal: "💬 **Question**: Two Sum problem\n\n---\n\n⭐️ **Answer**: I can solve this efficiently in one pass using a hash map to store each number and its index as I iterate through the array. This allows me to check if the complement of the current number already exists in the map in constant time.\n\n**🔑 Key Steps**: Initialize an empty hash map to store numbers and their indices. Iterate through the array and calculate the complement for each element by subtracting it from the target. Check if the complement exists in the map; if it does, return its index along with the current index. Otherwise, store the current number and its index in the map and continue.\n\n**💻 Code**:\n```python\ndef twoSum(nums, target):\n    # Map to store the value and its corresponding index\n    seen = {}\n    \n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        complement = target - num\n        # Check if complement is already in our map\n        if complement in seen:\n            return [seen[complement], i]\n        # Otherwise, add the current number to the map\n        seen[num] = i\n```\n\n**💡 Explanation**: This approach reduces the time complexity from O(n^2) of a brute-force nested loop down to O(n) by trading space for speed. We traverse the list containing n elements only once, and hash map lookups take O(1) average time, resulting in an O(n) space complexity."
