              formal: "💬 **Question**: Two Sum (find indices of two numbers that add up to target)\n\n---\n\n⭐️ **Answer**: I'd solve this efficiently using a hash map. Instead of using a nested loop which would take $O(n^2)$ time, we can iterate through the array once and store each number's complement—which is the target minus the current number—along with its index in the hash map. As we visit each element, we check if it already exists in our hash map; if it does, we've found our pair and can immediately return its index and the current index.\n\n**🔑 Key Steps**: Initialize an empty hash map to store numbers and their indices. Iterate through the array and calculate the complement for each element. Check if the complement is already in the hash map; if so, return the stored index and the current index. Otherwise, store the current number and its index in the hash map and continue.\n\n**💻 Code**:\n```python\ndef twoSum(nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:\n    # Map to store the value and its corresponding index\n    num_map = {}\n    \n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        complement = target - num\n        \n        # Check if complement exists in our map\n        if complement in num_map:\n            return [num_map[complement], i]\n            \n        # Otherwise, add the current number and index to the map\n        num_map[num] = i\n        \n    return []\n```\n\n**💡 Explanation**: The approach uses a single pass hash map to achieve an optimal time complexity of $O(n)$ because lookups in a hash map take $O(1)$ time on average. The space complexity is also $O(n)$ in the worst case, as we store up to $n$ elements in the hash map when no solution is found early or when the pair is near the end of the array."
